scene, drm, output: wire post-blend encode into scanout and composition

Completes the nvidia color pipeline path (spicy smithay a6a4be6d, niri
96c7e4a7/31b38918; knowledge only, no code reuse): on outputs whose plane
pipelines cannot express a terminal encode, the scene now scans out or
composes with a linear-retargeted recipe and puts the encode on the CRTC
GAMMA_LUT via the supplied-transform path.

Capability is derived from discovery, not vendor names: an output needs
post-blend when no pipeline ends in a curve advertising an inverse EOTF
and the CRTC has a GAMMA_LUT. The scanout path requires a fullscreen
opaque buffer (the gamma LUT covers the whole output); the composed FP16
offload retargets the combined output transform the same way. The encode
LUT is cached per scene output by transfer function, sink peak (max_cll,
else the curve's default max) and gamma size; linear_max = peak / 80 in
the PQ 125 scale.

Gamma ownership is scene state: while a frame offloads the encode the
compositor owns the LUT and the gamma-control attempt is skipped (ramps
defer naturally via gamma_lut_changed); the first frame without the
offload commits the reset, and a pending ramp applies on that same frame.
Mode flips damage the whole output. The verified-key and scanout retry
caches distinguish post-blend recipes through their fingerprints.

The cursor plane goes through the CRTC gamma LUT, so post-blend frames
need it in normalized linear light: an output addon selects a matrix-only
cursor transform (1.0 = output peak) and mode changes re-render cursor
buffers. The backend replaces the blanket cursor-visible rejection with
a mode-match check: post-blend recipes require the linearized cursor,
plane-only conversions require the wire-encoded one, so plane-only
conversion now also works with a visible hardware cursor.

